Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org # 2.6.29+
Signed-off-by: Eric Biggers <ebiggers@google.com>
Signed-off-by: David Howells <dhowells@redhat.com>

Here is the root cause:
The bug was caused by the `keyctl_set_reqkey_keyring()` function allowing the creation of new thread keyrings without checking if one already existed. This led to a situation where unprivileged users could exhaust kernel memory by repeatedly invoking this function, resulting in a memory leak of thread keyrings. The lack of checks in the `install_thread_keyring_to_cred()` function allowed for the continuous allocation of new keyrings, exacerbating the issue.

Here is the patch:
